      "side": 1,
      "message": "Not blocking: this reverses behaviour that is built, and the text doesn\u0027t say so.\n\nToday a bridge\u0027s panic becomes a catchable Scheme error (`bridge.rs:910`, \"A panic becomes an error naming the function\"), and only a primitive\u0027s panic poisons. That one returns `Error::Poisoned` rather than re-raising (`vm.rs:1837`). The comment there gives the reason: a bridge\u0027s panic \"cannot touch the heap\". The new rule poisons for both and re-raises, but this item reads as built, with no \"Not built yet\".\n\nOn the merits: an async bridge\u0027s future holds no Scheme value and runs outside the lock, so its panic can\u0027t corrupt anything. On a `SharedIsolate` serving many requests, one bridge\u0027s `unwrap` on bad input now poisons every call in flight. \"For one rule\" may be worth that, but the record should say why it outweighs the property the code relies on today.",
      "revId": "dc833695aa17e375a7f704d4fe98925e5cd07ab2",
