      "message": "A `std::sync::Mutex` taken inside `poll` blocks the executor thread of every call that waits for it.\n\n\"A wait for it lasts one poll\", but one poll is the other call\u0027s whole synchronous stretch: `poll_budget` defaults to `None` (item 9), so a poll runs until its call finishes or reaches an async bridge. Meanwhile each other call\u0027s poll sits in `lock()` on its own worker thread. With N concurrent requests on one `SharedIsolate`, up to N−1 Tokio workers are blocked at once, which is the \"Scheme holding a worker thread\" the Context sets out to prevent. Even with the recommended 4,096 budget, each waiting thread blocks for 50 to 200 µs per poll, again and again.\n\nFix: hand the isolate over without blocking, using only `std`. A poll does `try_lock`; on contention it registers its waker in the shared state\u0027s waiter queue and returns `Pending`, and the poll that unlocks wakes the next waiter. The synchronous `with`, `call` and `Call::run()` can keep blocking, since a synchronous caller chose to wait.",
      "revId": "dc833695aa17e375a7f704d4fe98925e5cd07ab2",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
