      "side": 1,
      "message": "Fuel doesn\u0027t bound work done in Rust, so neither a fuel limit nor the poll budget bounds time.\n\n\"A call into Rust spends none\" also covers Vali\u0027s own primitives, and many of them do work that grows with their input. In a debug build:\n\n```\n(vector-fill! v 1)                  ; 20M elements: 0.99 s, about one unit\n(expt 3 2000000)                    ; 0.58 s, about one unit\n(let loop ((i 0)) ... (vector-set! v i 2) ...)  ; 20M iterations: 7.7 s, 20M units\n```\n\nThe same work costs about one unit in a primitive and 20M units in Scheme. A metered call limited to N units can do about N × (the largest input the memory limit allows) work by calling `vector-fill!` or `expt` in a loop. A `poll_budget` of 4,096 likewise allows 4,096 such calls per poll, so item 12\u0027s \"about 50 to 200 microseconds\" holds only for code that doesn\u0027t call them. The Consequences line \"code that must be bounded runs on a metered isolate or with a `poll_budget`\" therefore doesn\u0027t hold for a sandbox.\n\nFix: make fuel a measure of work, not of Scheme steps. A primitive whose cost grows with its input charges for it, for example one unit per k elements, bytes or bignum limbs, and checks the deadline before a long one. Bridges get a way to charge too, as Wasmtime lets host functions consume fuel. Or, at the least, the record should say that fuel bounds Scheme steps only, and what bounds a single primitive.",
      "revId": "dc833695aa17e375a7f704d4fe98925e5cd07ab2",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
