      "side": 1,
      "message": "A `SchemeFn` callback lets a fuel-limited call escape its limit.\n\nThis item makes a `SchemeFn` callback \"a new `Call` with its own\" budget, and a new `Call`\u0027s default `fuel` is `None`, unlimited. So a call bound with `fuel: Some(n)` can pass `(lambda () (let loop () (loop)))` to any async bridge that takes a `SchemeFn`, such as a row-callback or event-handler bridge. The bridge\u0027s future then runs it with no limit, and only the optional isolate-wide cap (item 11) stops it. That is the same escape this item closes for `(vali async)` futures.\n\nFix: a `SchemeFn` converted during a call takes that call\u0027s `CallOptions` and charges its fuel to that call while the call lives, or to the isolate cap afterwards. Alternatively, this item and the Consequences should say that per-call limits do not cover callbacks, and that a sandboxing host must set the cap.",
      "revId": "dc833695aa17e375a7f704d4fe98925e5cd07ab2",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
