      "message": "Dropping a `Call` needs the isolate, and the record doesn\u0027t say how a drop reaches it.\n\n- **Owned isolate:** a `Call` is driven with `run_on(\u0026mut isolate)` and holds no reference to it. When a host drops a `Call` that came back in `OutOfFuel`, its parked task is in `Cx`\u0027s table (item 21), and `Drop` can\u0027t reach it to do this item\u0027s teardown (`release_frames`, releasing the `%catch` handlers, cutting the stack).\n- **Shared isolate:** `Drop` can reach the isolate through the `Arc`, but taking the lock blocks the dropping thread while another call polls. A Tokio timeout firing (item 7) is that case. Worse, a drop under the lock deadlocks: for example in a bridge\u0027s synchronous part that drops a `SchemeFn`\u0027s `Call` it held, or uses `?` on a `Stopped`. Item 4\u0027s thread-local flag can\u0027t turn a `Drop` into a condition.\n\nFix: do what a dropped `Handle` already does (`handle.rs:559`). `Drop` marks the task discarded and pushes it on a lock-free list, and the isolate tears it down at its next entry or poll. The memory and stack limits keep counting it until then. The same question decides where a parked task\u0027s bridge future lives. Item 19 puts it in `Task::awaiting`, inside `Cx` behind the lock, while item 20 polls it outside the lock. It belongs with the `Call`.",
      "revId": "dc833695aa17e375a7f704d4fe98925e5cd07ab2",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
